{:tier :glm-5.2, :verse 19, :comments ["<b>And the angel of Elohim journeyed</b> Rabbi Abraham ibn Ezra said (on Exodus 14:19): <b>the angel of Elohim</b> is the great prince who goes in the cloud, and he is the one concerning whom it is written, \"And YHWH went before them by day.\" And when this angel journeyed, <b>who went before the camp of Israel</b>, <b>and went behind them</b>, the pillar of cloud journeyed with him. <b>And it was the cloud and the darkness</b> between camp and camp; <b>and it lit up</b> for Israel <b>the night</b> with the pillar of fire as it did on the rest of the nights, in order to cross the sea, for they crossed at night. And in my opinion, this that Scripture says, <b>and the angel of Elohim journeyed</b>, was at the beginning of the night. <b>And the angel of Elohim who went before the camp of Israel</b> is an allusion to the heavenly court of the Holy One, blessed be He, for the attribute of judgment is called \"angel\" in various places in Scripture, and it is the one that dwells in the pillar of fire and goes before them at night to give them light; therefore He mentioned <b>Elohim</b>. And it is possible that it is not connected [to what follows], but rather it is an explanation.\n\nI saw in the Mekhilta of Rabbi Shimon bar Yochai: Rabbi Yonatan ben Yochai asked Rabbi Shimon bar Yochai, Why is it written everywhere \"the angel of YHWH,\" but here \"the angel of Elohim\"? He said to him: \"Elohim\" everywhere means only a judge, etc. They alluded to what we have said. So now the pillar of fire journeyed from before the camp of Israel and went behind them, and also <b>the pillar of cloud journeyed from before them and stood behind them</b>, and so both pillars were behind them. He then returned to explain that this pillar of cloud came <b>between the camp of Egypt and the camp of Israel</b>, to say that the pillar of cloud did not intervene between the pillar of fire and the camp of Israel, but it intervened between the camp of Egypt and the pillar of fire. <b>And it was the cloud and the darkness</b> between camp and camp, and there was light for Israel from the pillar of fire even though it was behind them, because it was on high and the pillar of cloud did not block it from them as it did for the Egyptians. This is the meaning of <b>and it lit up the night</b> — for it lit up the night for them, but not as on the other nights when they traveled on the way, for it was not going before them. And this was so because had the pillar of fire gone before Israel as on the other nights, with the pillar of cloud between Egypt and Israel, Israel would have hurried forward and the Egyptians would not have seen nor followed them. But now Israel walked slowly and the distance between them was not great, and the Egyptians would see the camp of Israel through the cloud and follow them, for they would see the fire through the cloud — except that they could not approach them because of the barrier of the two pillars. This is the meaning of <b>and the one did not come near the other all night</b>.\n\n<b>And it came to pass in the morning watch, and YHWH looked upon the camp</b>, etc. — for He removed the pillar of fire from the camp of Israel as was the practice every day, and placed it by day looking down upon the camp of Egypt, between them and the pillar of cloud that Israel would use by day. <b>And He threw the camp of Egypt into confusion</b> — when the pillar of fire looked down upon them, for its great heat would reach them, and \"a flame kindles the wicked\" (Psalms 106:18). I have already explained in the account of Creation (Genesis 1:4) that the foundation of fire is called darkness, and <b>\"the cloud and the darkness\"</b> is a pillar of fire and cloud, but the pillar of fire would give light to all Israel in its column, and now it darkened for the Egyptians because it came within the pillar of cloud, which darkened over it like the sun when a cloud covers it. So He did everything with these two pillars. This is what is correct in these verses:"], :book :exodus, :voice :ramban, :lang :en, :chapter 14, :notes {0 "Ramban cites Ibn Ezra's name via the abbreviation ר\"א at the opening; expanded to Rabbi Abraham ibn Ezra per his identification. The Mekhilta citation breaks off mid-thought with 'וכו׳' (etc.), rendered here as such."}, :source-witnesses [{"versionTitle" "Sefaria community Hebrew (PD)", "language" "he"}], :timestamp "2026-07-22T04:17:05.450841092Z", :translated-from :he, :model "glm-5.2"}